From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ghaf contributors <ghaf@tii.ae>
Date: Mon, 8 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] journal: tolerate repeated same-epoch FSS tags in
 --verify

Vendored, pending upstream (Ghaf ticket SSRCSP-8820). Review with the FSS
subsystem owner.

`journal_file_verify()` with SEALED_CONTINUOUS only tolerates a single
repeated tag epoch (the boundary repeat at tag #2). A forward wall-clock
correction makes journald close/rotate/restart a sealed file several times
inside one FSPRG interval -- each close writes a TAG at the same, not-yet-
evolved epoch (`journal_file_append_tag()` stamps
`FSPRG_GetEpoch(f->fsprg_state)` unconditionally). From the third such TAG
onward `journalctl --verify` rejects the file permanently:

    NNNNNN: Epoch sequence not continuous (E vs E)
    File corruption detected at .../system@....journal:OFF ...
    FAIL: .../system@....journal (Bad message)

The equal operands are diagnostic: this is a repeat, never a rollback.

Relax the check to tolerate any number of consecutive tags recording the
same epoch. Every such TAG remains fully authenticated by checks that do
not depend on epoch continuity:

  * `o->tag.seqnum != n_tags + 1` -> fail: tag seqnums stay dense and
    strictly forward, so no TAG can be dropped, replayed or reordered;
  * the per-TAG HMAC is computed over that TAG's own contiguous,
    non-overlapping `[last_tag, p]` byte range (the verify loop walks
    offsets monotonically) under the FSPRG key for `o->tag.epoch`
    (`journal_file_fsprg_seek()`); a forged TAG or any tampered byte in
    its range -> "Tag failed verification";
  * entry realtimes in the range must fall inside
    `[fss_start + epoch*interval, +interval)` and no entry may predate the
    previous tag ("Older entry after newer tag").

The only property SEALED_CONTINUOUS adds over the non-continuous branch is
refusing a forward gap (`epoch > last_epoch + 1`); that is kept. A
backward epoch (`epoch < last_epoch`) still fails, so forward security is
unchanged: a repeated-epoch TAG is sealed with the key already in use for
that interval and grants an attacker nothing they could not already do
with that key.

`last_epoch` still only ever advances by 0 or +1, so the next real
interval-boundary tag (`epoch == last_epoch + 1`) continues to pass.
---
 src/libsystemd/sd-journal/journal-verify.c | 15 +++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/libsystemd/sd-journal/journal-verify.c b/src/libsystemd/sd-journal/journal-verify.c
--- a/src/libsystemd/sd-journal/journal-verify.c
+++ b/src/libsystemd/sd-journal/journal-verify.c
@@ -1135,8 +1135,19 @@
                         }

                         if (JOURNAL_HEADER_SEALED_CONTINUOUS(f->header)) {
-                                if (!(n_tags == 0 || (n_tags == 1 && le64toh(o->tag.epoch) == last_epoch)
-                                      || le64toh(o->tag.epoch) == last_epoch + 1)) {
+                                /* SSRCSP-8820: tolerate any number of consecutive tags that
+                                 * record the same epoch, not only a single boundary repeat.
+                                 * A forward wall-clock correction makes journald close/rotate
+                                 * a sealed file several times inside one FSPRG interval, each
+                                 * close writing a TAG at the same unevolved epoch. Every such
+                                 * TAG is still authenticated below: dense forward seqnum
+                                 * (checked above), a per-TAG HMAC over its own contiguous
+                                 * [last_tag, p] range under the epoch-correct FSPRG key, and
+                                 * entry realtimes confined to the epoch window. A backward
+                                 * epoch (epoch < last_epoch) and a forward gap
+                                 * (epoch > last_epoch + 1) both still fail. */
+                                if (!(n_tags == 0 || le64toh(o->tag.epoch) == last_epoch
+                                      || le64toh(o->tag.epoch) == last_epoch + 1)) {
                                         error(p,
                                               "Epoch sequence not continuous (%"PRIu64" vs %"PRIu64")",
                                               le64toh(o->tag.epoch),
--
